  {
    t: "VPC networks: auto vs custom mode, subnets, firewall rules, Shared VPC and VPC Network Peering",
    body: [
      "A Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) network in Google Cloud is global: one VPC can have subnets in many regions, and VMs in different regions of the same VPC can talk over internal IPs without extra setup. Subnets are regional and each has a primary IPv4 range, plus optional secondary ranges used for things like GKE pod and service IPs.",
      "When you create a VPC you choose a subnet mode. In auto mode, Google creates one subnet in every region automatically from the 10.128.0.0/9 block, and adds new regions as they launch. The default network that new projects often have is an auto mode VPC with permissive firewall rules. In custom mode, you create only the subnets you want with the ranges you choose. Custom mode is recommended for production because it avoids overlapping with on-premises or other networks you might connect later. You can convert an auto mode network to custom, but not back.",
      "VPC firewall rules are stateful and apply to VM instances. Each rule has a direction (ingress or egress), an action (allow or deny), a priority from 0 to 65535 where lower numbers win, protocols and ports, a source or destination range, and targets: all instances, instances with a network tag, or instances running as a service account. Every VPC has implied rules that deny all ingress and allow all egress. For example: `gcloud compute firewall-rules create allow-web --network=prod --direction=INGRESS --action=ALLOW --rules=tcp:80,tcp:443 --source-ranges=0.0.0.0/0 --target-tags=web`. Hierarchical firewall policies at the organization or folder level and network firewall policies add central control.",
      "Shared VPC lets a host project own a VPC network while service projects create resources in its subnets. A central network team manages subnets, routes and firewalls in the host project, and app teams get Compute Network User on specific subnets. This is the answer for centralized network control across many projects in one organization.",
      "VPC Network Peering connects two VPC networks, in the same or different projects or organizations, so they exchange internal routes privately. Each side remains administered separately, subnet ranges must not overlap, and peering isn't transitive: if A peers with B and B with C, A can't reach C through B."
    ],
    terms: [["Custom mode VPC", "A VPC where you create subnets and choose their ranges yourself."], ["Firewall rule priority", "A number from 0 to 65535 where the lowest-numbered matching rule takes effect."], ["Shared VPC", "A model where service projects use subnets from a centrally managed host project's VPC."], ["VPC Network Peering", "A private, non-transitive connection between two VPC networks that exchanges subnet routes."]],
    example: "A company builds a custom mode VPC in a Shared VPC host project with subnets in us-east1 and europe-west1 that don't overlap its data center ranges. Five app teams attach their service projects, and a firewall rule allows tcp:443 only to VMs with the tag web.",
    tip: "Central team manages the network for many projects: Shared VPC. Connecting two separately managed VPCs: peering. Peering isn't transitive and ranges can't overlap. Lower priority numbers win.",
    check: [["Is a VPC network regional or global in Google Cloud?", "Global. Its subnets are regional."], ["Which implied firewall rules does every VPC have?", "Deny all ingress and allow all egress, at the lowest priority."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Load balancing, Cloud NAT and hybrid connectivity with Cloud VPN and Cloud Interconnect",
    body: [
      "Cloud Load Balancing is a family of managed load balancers. Pick one by asking three questions: is the traffic HTTP(S) (layer 7) or other TCP/UDP (layer 4), is it from the internet (external) or from inside your network (internal), and is it global or regional?",
      "Application Load Balancers are layer 7 proxies for HTTP and HTTPS. The global external Application Load Balancer gives one anycast IP address worldwide, routes by host and path with URL maps, terminates TLS with Google-managed or your own certificates, and integrates with Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor. Regional external and internal Application Load Balancers serve traffic in one region. Network Load Balancers are layer 4. Proxy Network Load Balancers terminate TCP (optionally with TLS offload); passthrough Network Load Balancers forward packets without proxying, so backends see the original client IP, and they support TCP, UDP and other protocols. Internal passthrough Network Load Balancers distribute traffic inside a VPC.",
      "All of them use health checks and backend services pointing at managed instance groups, network endpoint groups (for GKE pods or serverless services like Cloud Run), or buckets for static content.",
      "Cloud NAT provides outbound internet access for resources without external IP addresses, such as private VMs and GKE nodes, while blocking unsolicited inbound connections. It's configured per region on a Cloud Router and doesn't use a proxy VM. Private Google Access, a subnet setting, lets VMs without external IPs reach Google APIs such as Cloud Storage without NAT.",
      "To connect on-premises networks, Cloud VPN creates IPsec tunnels over the internet. HA VPN uses two interfaces and, with two tunnels configured correctly, offers a 99.99% availability SLA; it uses Cloud Router to exchange routes dynamically with BGP. VPN is quick to set up but limited by internet performance. Cloud Interconnect provides private, high-bandwidth connections: Dedicated Interconnect is a direct physical link to Google in a colocation facility, and Partner Interconnect goes through a service provider when you can't reach a Google facility or need less bandwidth. Interconnect traffic doesn't cross the public internet, but it isn't encrypted by default. Direct and carrier peering reach Google's public services, such as Workspace, not your VPC."
    ],
    terms: [["Application Load Balancer", "A layer 7 proxy load balancer for HTTP(S) with URL-based routing."], ["Passthrough Network Load Balancer", "A layer 4 load balancer that forwards packets and preserves client IP addresses."], ["Cloud NAT", "Managed network address translation that gives private resources outbound internet access."], ["HA VPN", "Highly available IPsec VPN to Google Cloud using two interfaces and BGP through Cloud Router."]],
    example: "A retailer serves its storefront through a global external Application Load Balancer with Cloud CDN, runs back-end VMs without external IPs that download updates through Cloud NAT, and connects its warehouses over HA VPN while a Partner Interconnect is being provisioned.",
    tip: "HTTP(S) plus global plus URL routing or CDN: global external Application Load Balancer. Private VMs needing outbound internet: Cloud NAT. Fast to set up over the internet: HA VPN. Private, high bandwidth: Interconnect.",
    check: [["Which load balancers preserve the client's source IP to the backend?", "Passthrough Network Load Balancers, because they don't proxy connections."], ["What does Cloud NAT need to be configured on?", "A Cloud Router in the region where the private resources are."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Infrastructure as code with Terraform and Cloud Marketplace solutions",
    body: [
      "Infrastructure as code (IaC) means describing your infrastructure in files that are stored in version control, reviewed like code and applied automatically. It gives you repeatable environments, peer review of changes and a history of who changed what. For Google Cloud, the most common tool is Terraform, an open source tool from HashiCorp, used with the Google Cloud provider.",
      "In Terraform you write declarative configuration in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL). For example, a `google_compute_instance` resource block describes a VM's name, zone, machine type and boot disk. The workflow is: `terraform init` downloads providers and modules; `terraform plan` compares your configuration with the recorded state and shows what will be created, changed or destroyed; `terraform apply` makes the changes; `terraform destroy` removes everything the configuration manages.",
      "Terraform keeps a state file that maps configuration to real resources. For teams, store state remotely in a Cloud Storage bucket (with Object Versioning on) using the gcs backend, so everyone shares one state and changes are locked while running. Don't keep state only on a laptop, and treat it as sensitive because it can contain secrets.",
      "Modules package reusable configuration, such as a standard VPC or a project factory. Google's Cloud Foundation Toolkit publishes modules that follow best practices. Terraform is preinstalled in Cloud Shell, and many console pages can show equivalent Terraform. Infrastructure Manager is a Google Cloud service that runs Terraform deployments for you and stores their state. Google's older Deployment Manager, which used YAML templates, has been deprecated in favor of Infrastructure Manager, so new work should use Terraform or Infrastructure Manager. Config Connector is another option: it lets you manage Google Cloud resources as Kubernetes objects from a GKE cluster.",
      "Cloud Marketplace offers prebuilt solutions from Google and partners, such as WordPress stacks, databases, security appliances and developer tools, that you can deploy in a few clicks. Some are free, some are billed through your Cloud Billing account under the vendor's license terms. Marketplace is the answer when a question asks for the quickest way to deploy a common third-party product."
    ],
    terms: [["Infrastructure as code", "Managing infrastructure through versioned, declarative configuration files."], ["terraform plan", "Shows the changes Terraform would make without applying them."], ["Terraform state", "A file that records which real resources correspond to the configuration."], ["Cloud Marketplace", "A catalog of ready-to-deploy solutions from Google and partners."]],
    example: "A platform team keeps its network, projects and GKE clusters in a git repository of Terraform modules. Each pull request runs terraform plan so reviewers see the exact changes, and merging runs terraform apply with state stored in a versioned Cloud Storage bucket.",
    tip: "'Preview changes before applying', 'version-controlled', 'reusable modules' all point to Terraform. 'Deploy a common third-party product quickly' points to Cloud Marketplace.",
    check: [["Where should a team store shared Terraform state on Google Cloud?", "In a Cloud Storage bucket using the gcs backend, ideally with Object Versioning enabled."], ["What does terraform plan do?", "It shows what would be created, changed or destroyed, without changing anything."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Managing Compute Engine VMs: start, stop, resize, SSH with OS Login and IAP TCP forwarding",
    body: [
      "Day-to-day VM management is mostly a handful of gcloud commands. `gcloud compute instances list` shows VMs with their zones, status and IPs. `gcloud compute instances stop web-1 --zone=us-central1-a` and `start` stop and start a VM; a stopped VM doesn't incur charges for vCPUs and memory, but you still pay for its disks and any reserved static IPs. `suspend` saves memory state to disk and `resume` brings it back. `reset` is a hard restart, like pulling the power cord.",
      "To change a VM's machine type, you must stop it first: `gcloud compute instances set-machine-type web-1 --machine-type=e2-standard-4`, then start it again. Disks can be enlarged while the VM runs (`gcloud compute disks resize`), after which you grow the file system inside the OS. You can also add labels, metadata such as startup scripts, and network tags without recreating the VM. Moving a VM to another zone or region generally means creating a new VM from a snapshot or machine image.",
      "For SSH, `gcloud compute ssh web-1` handles keys for you. There are two ways keys get authorized. With metadata-based SSH keys, public keys are stored in project or instance metadata, and anyone whose key is there can log in until someone removes it. With OS Login, Linux accounts are tied to Google identities: access is controlled by IAM roles such as Compute OS Login (regular user) or Compute OS Admin Login (sudo), it can require two-step verification, and removing a person's role or disabling their account removes their access. Enable it with the metadata key `enable-oslogin=TRUE` on the project or instance. OS Login is recommended for organizations.",
      "Many VMs shouldn't have external IP addresses. To reach them without a bastion host or VPN, use Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) TCP forwarding: `gcloud compute ssh web-1 --tunnel-through-iap`. IAP wraps the SSH connection in HTTPS and checks the user's IAM permission (IAP-secured Tunnel User) before forwarding traffic. Your firewall must allow ingress on tcp:22 (or 3389 for RDP) from IAP's range, 35.235.240.0/20. The same technique works for RDP to Windows VMs.",
      "When SSH itself is broken, the serial console lets you interact with the VM's boot process and login prompt, if enabled. Serial port output (`gcloud compute instances get-serial-port-output`) shows boot logs and is safe to read without enabling interactive access."
    ],
    terms: [["OS Login", "A feature that ties Linux accounts on VMs to Google identities and controls SSH access with IAM roles."], ["IAP TCP forwarding", "Tunneling SSH, RDP or other TCP traffic through Identity-Aware Proxy to VMs without external IPs."], ["35.235.240.0/20", "The source range IAP TCP forwarding uses, which firewall rules must allow."], ["Serial console", "Interactive access to a VM's serial port for troubleshooting when normal login fails."]],
    example: "A security team removes all external IPs from their VMs, enables OS Login at the project level and grants the ops group Compute OS Admin Login and IAP-secured Tunnel User. Admins now connect with `gcloud compute ssh --tunnel-through-iap`, and when someone leaves the company their access ends as soon as their account is disabled.",
    tip: "No external IP and no bastion: IAP TCP forwarding with a firewall rule for 35.235.240.0/20. Access tied to IAM and removed automatically: OS Login. Changing the machine type requires stopping the VM.",
    check: [["What must you do before changing a VM's machine type?", "Stop the VM, change the type, then start it again."], ["Which IAM role lets a user connect through IAP TCP forwarding?", "IAP-secured Tunnel User, together with a firewall rule allowing 35.235.240.0/20."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Snapshots, snapshot schedules, custom images and image families",
    body: [
      "Compute Engine has several ways to capture disks and VMs, and each has a different purpose. Knowing which to use is a common exam task.",
      "A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a single persistent disk or Hyperdisk. Snapshots are incremental: the first contains all the data, and later ones store only the blocks that changed, which keeps them cheap. They're stored in Cloud Storage-backed locations (multi-regional by default, or a region you choose) and can be restored to a new disk in any zone, which is also a way to move a disk between zones or regions. Create one with `gcloud compute snapshots create web-snap --source-disk=web-1 --source-disk-zone=us-central1-a`. For application-consistent backups, flush or pause writes first; otherwise a snapshot is crash-consistent. Instant snapshots and archive snapshots are variations for fast in-zone rollback and cheaper long-term retention.",
      "A snapshot schedule is a resource policy that creates snapshots automatically, for example daily at 02:00, and deletes them after a retention period such as 14 days. You create the schedule with `gcloud compute resource-policies create snapshot-schedule` and attach it to disks with `gcloud compute disks add-resource-policies`. This is the no-scripting way to back up VMs. For centralized, policy-based backups with reporting across many resources, Google also offers the Backup and DR service.",
      "A custom image is a boot disk template. You create one from a disk, snapshot or another image, typically after installing and hardening software, and then create many VMs or instance templates from it. Images can be shared with other projects through IAM. Grouping images in an image family lets instance templates reference the family, so new VMs automatically use the latest image, and you can deprecate a bad image to roll back to the previous one.",
      "A machine image captures an entire VM: its configuration (machine type, metadata, network settings) and all its disks. It's useful for cloning a whole VM or backing it up with its settings, whereas an image contains only one disk."
    ],
    terms: [["Snapshot", "An incremental, point-in-time backup of one disk that can be restored to a new disk."], ["Snapshot schedule", "A resource policy that creates and deletes disk snapshots automatically on a schedule."], ["Custom image", "A boot disk template made from your own disk, used to create identical VMs."], ["Machine image", "A capture of a whole VM, including its configuration and all its disks."]],
    example: "An operations team attaches a daily snapshot schedule with 14-day retention to every production disk. Each month they build a patched, hardened custom image in the family web-base, and their instance templates reference the family so new VMs always start from the latest build.",
    tip: "Backup of a disk: snapshot. Automatic backups without scripts: snapshot schedule. Template for many identical VMs: custom image in an image family. Whole VM with its settings: machine image.",
    check: [["Why are successive snapshots of the same disk cheaper than the first?", "They're incremental, storing only blocks that changed since the previous snapshot."], ["How can you move a disk's data to another region?", "Take a snapshot and create a new disk from it in the target region."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Managing GKE: scaling Deployments, node pool autoscaling, rollouts and rollbacks, and cluster upgrades",
    body: [
      "Running GKE means managing two layers of capacity: pods and nodes. Keep them straight, because exam distractors mix them up.",
      "At the pod layer, you scale a Deployment manually with `kubectl scale deployment/web --replicas=8`, or automatically with a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA), for example `kubectl autoscale deployment web --cpu-percent=60 --min=2 --max=20`. The HPA changes the number of pod replicas based on CPU, memory or custom metrics. The Vertical Pod Autoscaler recommends or sets pod CPU and memory requests instead of the replica count.",
      "At the node layer in a Standard cluster, the cluster autoscaler adds nodes when pods are Pending because no node has room for them, and removes underused nodes. You enable it per node pool: `gcloud container clusters update prod --enable-autoscaling --node-pool=default-pool --min-nodes=1 --max-nodes=10 --region=us-central1`. You can also resize a pool manually with `gcloud container clusters resize`. Node auto-provisioning can create new node pools automatically. In Autopilot, Google handles all node scaling for you.",
      "Updating an app is a rollout. Change the image with `kubectl set image deployment/web web=REPO/web:2.0` or by applying new YAML, and Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet and shifts pods gradually according to the Deployment's maxSurge and maxUnavailable. Watch it with `kubectl rollout status deployment/web`, list past versions with `kubectl rollout history`, and if the new version misbehaves, `kubectl rollout undo deployment/web` returns to the previous revision.",
      "Clusters themselves need upgrades. Google upgrades the control plane automatically, and nodes are upgraded automatically when node auto-upgrade is on, which is the default and required in Autopilot. Release channels (Rapid, Regular, Stable) decide how quickly new versions arrive; maintenance windows and exclusions control when upgrades may happen. Surge upgrades replace nodes a few at a time, and PodDisruptionBudgets tell Kubernetes how many replicas of an app must stay available while nodes are drained. Node auto-repair recreates nodes that fail health checks.",
      "To see what's happening, use `kubectl get pods`, `kubectl describe` for events such as scheduling failures, and the GKE pages in the console, which show workloads, logs and metrics from Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring."
    ],
    terms: [["Horizontal Pod Autoscaler", "Kubernetes object that changes a workload's number of pod replicas based on metrics."], ["Cluster autoscaler", "GKE feature that adds or removes nodes in a node pool based on pending pods and utilization."], ["kubectl rollout undo", "Reverts a Deployment to its previous revision."], ["Release channel", "A GKE setting that controls how quickly a cluster receives new Kubernetes versions."]],
    example: "During a sale, a retailer's HPA scales the checkout Deployment from 4 to 30 pods. Several go Pending, so the cluster autoscaler adds nodes to the pool within minutes. After a buggy release later that day, the on-call engineer runs `kubectl rollout undo deployment/checkout` and error rates return to normal.",
    tip: "More pods: kubectl scale or HPA. More nodes: cluster autoscaler or resize the node pool. Bad release: kubectl rollout undo. Don't autoscale GKE's underlying instance groups directly.",
    check: [["Pods are stuck in Pending because nodes are full. Which feature adds nodes automatically?", "The cluster autoscaler, enabled on the node pool (or Autopilot, which does it for you)."], ["Which command shows a Deployment's previous revisions?", "`kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME`."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Managing Cloud Run: revisions, traffic splitting, minimum and maximum instances",
    body: [
      "Every deployment to a Cloud Run service creates a new immutable revision, named something like web-00007-abc. Revisions keep their image and settings, so you can move traffic back to an earlier one at any time. By default, a new revision receives 100% of traffic as soon as it's healthy.",
      "Traffic splitting lets you release gradually. Deploy the new revision without sending it traffic using `gcloud run deploy web --image=... --no-traffic --tag=green`, which gives it a tagged URL (the tag, three hyphens and the service's hostname) for testing. Then move traffic with `gcloud run services update-traffic web --to-revisions=web-00007-abc=10,web-00006-def=90` for a 10% canary, and later `--to-latest` to send everything to the newest revision. Rolling back is just moving traffic to the previous revision; no rebuild is needed.",
      "Scaling is automatic, but you control its limits. Maximum instances caps how many container instances can run, which protects downstream systems such as a database with a limited connection pool and limits cost. Minimum instances keeps a number of instances warm even with no traffic, which reduces cold-start latency (the delay while a new instance starts and initializes) at the cost of paying for idle instances. Concurrency sets how many requests one instance handles at once; the default is 80, and setting it to 1 suits code that isn't thread-safe but increases the number of instances needed.",
      "Other settings you'll adjust include CPU and memory per instance, request timeout, environment variables and secrets, the service account the service runs as, and billing mode: with request-based billing CPU is allocated only during requests, while instance-based billing (CPU always allocated) suits background work between requests.",
      "To observe a service, the Cloud Run console shows request counts, latencies, instance counts and logs for each revision. `gcloud run services describe web` shows the current traffic split, and `gcloud run revisions list` shows all revisions."
    ],
    terms: [["Revision", "An immutable version of a Cloud Run service created by each deployment."], ["Traffic split", "Percentages of requests sent to different revisions of one service."], ["Minimum instances", "A setting that keeps instances warm to reduce cold starts, billed while idle."], ["Cold start", "The delay when Cloud Run starts a new container instance to handle a request."]],
    example: "A bank's API team deploys a new revision with --no-traffic, runs tests against its tagged URL, then sends 5% of traffic to it. After an hour of clean error metrics they move to 50% and then 100%. They keep minimum instances at 2 so customers never hit a cold start during business hours.",
    tip: "Gradual release on Cloud Run is traffic splitting between revisions of the same service. Slow first request is fixed with minimum instances; protecting a database is done with maximum instances.",
    check: [["How do you roll back a Cloud Run service to the previous version?", "Send 100% of traffic to the previous revision with gcloud run services update-traffic (or the console)."], ["What's the trade-off of setting minimum instances above zero?", "Fewer cold starts, but you pay for those instances even when there is no traffic."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Managing Cloud Storage objects with gcloud storage and moving data with Storage Transfer Service",
    body: [
      "The `gcloud storage` command group is the current CLI for Cloud Storage and replaces most uses of the older `gsutil`. Buckets and objects are addressed with gs:// URLs. Common commands: `gcloud storage ls gs://my-bucket` lists objects; `gcloud storage cp report.csv gs://my-bucket/reports/` uploads; `gcloud storage cp -r ./site gs://my-bucket` copies a folder; `gcloud storage mv` moves or renames; `gcloud storage rm gs://my-bucket/old/**` deletes; and `gcloud storage rsync` synchronizes a directory with a bucket prefix. Large transfers are parallelized automatically.",
      "You can change an object's storage class by rewriting it, for example `gcloud storage objects update gs://my-bucket/file --storage-class=COLDLINE`, but for many objects a lifecycle rule is better. Bucket settings are managed with `gcloud storage buckets update`, for example to change the default storage class, add labels, or enable versioning with `--versioning`. Changing a bucket's default class affects only new objects.",
      "Remember that Cloud Storage has no real folders; names such as reports/2026/jan.csv just contain slashes, and the console displays them as folders. Objects are immutable: an update writes a new object (or a new version if versioning is on).",
      "For large or recurring transfers, use Storage Transfer Service. It's a managed service that copies data into Cloud Storage from other clouds (Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage), from HTTP/HTTPS URL lists, from other Cloud Storage buckets, and from on-premises file systems using agents you install. Jobs can run once or on a schedule, copy only changed files, delete source files after transfer, and report progress and errors. That makes it the right choice for moving terabytes from another cloud or keeping two buckets in sync nightly.",
      "For very large datasets with limited network bandwidth, Transfer Appliance is a physical storage device that Google ships to you; you load data onto it and ship it back. As a rough guide, if uploading over your network would take weeks, consider the appliance. BigQuery Data Transfer Service is different again: it loads data into BigQuery from sources such as SaaS apps and scheduled queries."
    ],
    terms: [["gcloud storage", "The current gcloud command group for managing buckets and objects."], ["gcloud storage rsync", "A command that makes a bucket prefix match a local directory or another location."], ["Storage Transfer Service", "A managed service for copying data into Cloud Storage from other clouds, URLs, buckets or on-premises."], ["Transfer Appliance", "A physical device shipped by Google for offline transfer of very large datasets."]],
    example: "A media company sets up a Storage Transfer Service job that copies new files from an Amazon S3 bucket to Cloud Storage every night at 01:00 and deletes nothing at the source. Their editors use `gcloud storage rsync` to push finished projects from a workstation to a bucket.",
    tip: "Other cloud to Cloud Storage, scheduled and managed: Storage Transfer Service. Hundreds of terabytes over a slow link: Transfer Appliance. Into BigQuery from SaaS: BigQuery Data Transfer Service.",
    check: [["Which command lists the objects in gs://logs-bucket?", "`gcloud storage ls gs://logs-bucket`."], ["Does changing a bucket's default storage class change existing objects?", "No. It applies to new objects; existing objects keep their class unless rewritten or changed by a lifecycle rule."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Operating databases: Cloud SQL backups, high availability and read replicas, and BigQuery dry runs",
    body: [
      "Cloud SQL handles much of database operations for you, but you choose the protection features. Automated backups run daily in a backup window you choose and are kept for a configurable number of days; you can also take on-demand backups before risky changes. Point-in-time recovery uses transaction logs (binary logs for MySQL, write-ahead logs for PostgreSQL) so you can restore to a specific moment, for example just before a bad DELETE. Restores go to a new or existing instance; they aren't instant failover.",
      "High availability (HA), also called the regional configuration, runs a primary instance in one zone and a standby in another zone of the same region, with synchronous replication of the storage. If the primary or its zone fails, Cloud SQL fails over automatically to the standby, and clients keep using the same IP address after a short interruption. The standby doesn't serve reads. HA protects against zone failures, not against a mistake like dropping a table, which backups and point-in-time recovery cover.",
      "Read replicas are copies of the primary that receive asynchronous replication and serve read-only queries. Use them to offload reporting or read-heavy traffic. Cross-region replicas also support disaster recovery: you can promote a replica to a standalone primary if a region is lost, though promotion is a manual, one-way action. Replicas need the primary to have backups and binary logging (for MySQL) enabled.",
      "Other operations include connecting securely with the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy or connectors (which use IAM and encrypt traffic without managing SSL certificates or allowlisting IPs), using private IP in your VPC, scaling up the machine type (which requires a restart), and scheduling maintenance windows.",
      "In BigQuery, the main operational concern is cost. With on-demand pricing you pay by bytes processed, so before running a large query run a dry run: `bq query --use_legacy_sql=false --dry_run 'SELECT ...'`, or look at the query validator in the console, which reports how many bytes the query will process. Select only needed columns, filter on partitioned columns and use clustering; adding LIMIT doesn't reduce bytes scanned for a normal table. You can also set a maximum bytes billed limit on a query or cap usage with custom quotas."
    ],
    terms: [["Point-in-time recovery", "Restoring a database to a specific moment using backups plus transaction logs."], ["Cloud SQL high availability", "A primary and a standby in different zones of a region with automatic failover."], ["Read replica", "An asynchronously updated copy that serves read-only traffic and can be promoted manually."], ["Dry run", "A BigQuery query validation that reports bytes to be processed without running or charging."]],
    example: "An e-commerce team runs Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL with HA for zone failures, point-in-time recovery for mistakes, and a read replica for its reporting tool. Analysts estimate every new BigQuery report with a dry run and add a partition filter when one query would have scanned 3 TB.",
    tip: "Automatic zone failover: HA. Offload reads: read replica. Undo a bad change: point-in-time recovery. Estimate BigQuery cost: dry run. LIMIT doesn't reduce bytes scanned.",
    check: [["Can a Cloud SQL HA standby serve read queries?", "No. It only takes over on failover; use read replicas for reads."], ["What does a BigQuery dry run tell you?", "Whether the query is valid and how many bytes it would process, without running it."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Operating networks: expanding subnets, static IP addresses and Cloud DNS",
    body: [
      "Networks change as workloads grow, and the exam expects you to know which changes can be made in place.",
      "Subnet ranges can be expanded but not shrunk. If 10.10.0.0/24 is running out of addresses, `gcloud compute networks subnets expand-ip-range app-subnet --region=us-central1 --prefix-length=22` grows it to 10.10.0.0/22 without affecting existing VMs, as long as the larger range doesn't overlap other subnets or peered networks. Each subnet reserves four addresses in its primary range (network, gateway, second-to-last and broadcast), so a /24 gives 252 usable addresses. You can also add secondary ranges, used for alias IPs such as GKE pods, and add new subnets in any region.",
      "IP addresses come in two lifetimes. Ephemeral external IPs are assigned when a VM starts and can change after it stops. Static external IPs are reserved to your project until you release them. Reserve one with `gcloud compute addresses create web-ip --region=us-central1` (regional, for VMs and regional load balancers) or `--global` (for global load balancers), or promote the ephemeral IP a VM already has so it never changes. Internal IPs can also be reserved as static so a server keeps the same private address. Reserved static external IPs that aren't attached to anything are billed, so release ones you don't use.",
      "Cloud DNS is a managed, authoritative DNS service. A public zone serves records for a domain on the internet, such as example.com; after creating it you update your registrar to use the name servers Cloud DNS assigns. A private zone answers only for queries from the VPC networks you authorize, which is handy for internal names like db.corp.internal. You manage records such as A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and TXT in the console or with `gcloud dns record-sets create`. DNS forwarding zones and server policies connect Cloud DNS with on-premises DNS in hybrid networks, and DNSSEC can be enabled for public zones.",
      "VMs also get automatic internal DNS names of the form VM_NAME.ZONE.c.PROJECT_ID.internal, so other VMs in the same network can reach them by name without managing records."
    ],
    terms: [["expand-ip-range", "The gcloud subcommand that enlarges a subnet's primary range in place."], ["Static external IP", "A reserved public address that stays with your project until released."], ["Private DNS zone", "A Cloud DNS zone visible only to authorized VPC networks."], ["Internal DNS", "Automatic names that let VMs in a network reach each other by name."]],
    example: "A SaaS company's app subnet fills up, so they expand it from /24 to /22 during business hours with no downtime. They promote the ephemeral IP of their SFTP server to static so customers' allowlists keep working, and create a private Cloud DNS zone so services find the database at db.prod.internal.",
    tip: "Subnets grow, never shrink. A changing public IP is fixed by reserving or promoting a static address. Internal-only names: private DNS zone.",
    check: [["How many usable addresses does a /24 subnet provide in Google Cloud?", "252, because four addresses in each primary range are reserved."], ["Do reserved static external IPs cost money when unused?", "Yes. Unattached reserved addresses are billed, so release them."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Cloud Monitoring: metrics, dashboards, alerting policies, uptime checks and the Ops Agent",
    body: [
      "Cloud Monitoring collects metrics, which are numeric measurements over time, from Google Cloud services, applications and agents. Many metrics are built in: Compute Engine CPU utilization, network bytes and disk operations; Cloud Run request counts and latencies; Cloud SQL connections; load balancer response codes. A metrics scope lets one project view metrics from several projects, so you can monitor a whole environment from one place.",
      "Some important VM metrics come from inside the guest OS and aren't collected by default, notably memory utilization and disk space used. To get them, install the Ops Agent on the VM. It collects system and application metrics and also sends logs to Cloud Logging, replacing the older separate Monitoring and Logging agents. You can install it on individual VMs or on fleets with an agent policy.",
      "Dashboards visualize metrics. Google provides predefined dashboards for many services, and you can build custom ones with charts, filters and grouping. Metrics Explorer lets you query any metric quickly, for example average CPU grouped by instance.",
      "Alerting policies notify people when something needs attention. A policy has one or more conditions, such as a metric threshold (CPU above 80% for 5 minutes), metric absence (no data), or a log match, and one or more notification channels such as email, SMS, the mobile app, Slack, PagerDuty, webhooks or Pub/Sub. When the condition is met, an incident opens and notifications go out; documentation in the policy tells responders what to do.",
      "Uptime checks test availability from outside. They send HTTP, HTTPS or TCP requests to a public URL, IP or resource from several locations around the world at a set interval, and can check the response content. Combined with an alerting policy, they tell you when users can't reach your site. For private endpoints, uptime checks can target internal resources through a supported configuration.",
      "Service monitoring builds on these with service-level objectives (SLOs), such as 99.9% of requests under 300 ms, and burn-rate alerts that warn when you're using up your error budget too fast."
    ],
    terms: [["Ops Agent", "Google's agent that collects guest OS metrics, such as memory and disk usage, and logs from VMs."], ["Alerting policy", "Conditions plus notification channels that open incidents when a metric or log condition is met."], ["Uptime check", "A probe of a URL, IP or resource from multiple global locations to test availability."], ["Metrics scope", "The set of projects whose metrics a scoping project can view together."]],
    example: "An operations team installs the Ops Agent on all VMs through an agent policy, adds a dashboard showing CPU, memory and disk per service, creates an alert when disk usage exceeds 85%, and adds an uptime check on the www.example.com home page that pages the on-call engineer if two regions fail.",
    tip: "Memory and disk-space metrics need the Ops Agent. 'Notify when a metric crosses a threshold' is an alerting policy. 'Is my website reachable from around the world' is an uptime check.",
    check: [["Why don't you see memory utilization for a new VM by default?", "Memory is measured inside the guest OS, so it requires the Ops Agent."], ["What are the two main parts of an alerting policy?", "Conditions that define when to alert, and notification channels that define who is told."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Cloud Logging: Logs Explorer, log-based metrics, log buckets and sinks",
    body: [
      "Cloud Logging collects log entries from Google Cloud services, audit logs, VMs running the Ops Agent, GKE containers, Cloud Run and your applications (anything written to standard output on serverless platforms, or through client libraries). Each entry has a timestamp, severity, a resource (such as a VM or Cloud Run revision), a log name, and a payload that is either text or structured JSON.",
      "Logs Explorer is where you search. You write queries in the Logging query language, for example `resource.type=\"gce_instance\" severity>=ERROR` or `textPayload:\"payment declined\"`, and narrow by time range. From the CLI, `gcloud logging read 'severity>=ERROR' --limit=20` returns entries. Log Analytics lets you query logs with SQL when a log bucket is upgraded for it.",
      "Log-based metrics turn log entries into Cloud Monitoring metrics. A counter metric counts entries matching a filter, such as how many times 'payment declined' appears; a distribution metric extracts numeric values, such as latencies, from entries. You can chart them and create alerting policies on them.",
      "Routing and storage are controlled by the Log Router. Every project has sinks that decide where each log entry goes. By default, the _Required bucket stores Admin Activity, System Event and some other audit logs for 400 days and can't be changed, and the _Default bucket stores most other logs for 30 days by default, which you can change. You can create your own log buckets with custom retention, including in other regions, and exclusion filters drop noisy logs you don't want to pay to store.",
      "User-defined sinks send copies of matching logs to other destinations: a Cloud Storage bucket for cheap long-term archiving, a BigQuery dataset for SQL analysis, a Pub/Sub topic for streaming to third-party tools such as a SIEM, a log bucket in another project, or a Google Cloud project. When you create a sink, Logging gives it a writer identity (a service account) that you must grant permission on the destination, for example BigQuery Data Editor on the dataset. Aggregated sinks, created on an organization or folder with `--include-children`, route logs from all projects beneath it, which is the standard way to centralize security logs."
    ],
    terms: [["Logs Explorer", "The console tool for querying and viewing log entries."], ["Log-based metric", "A Cloud Monitoring metric derived from log entries that match a filter."], ["Sink", "A Log Router rule that sends matching log entries to a destination such as BigQuery or Pub/Sub."], ["Aggregated sink", "A sink on an organization or folder that routes logs from all child projects."]],
    example: "A company creates an aggregated sink on its organization that sends all audit logs to a BigQuery dataset in a security project, and another that streams them to Pub/Sub for its SIEM. Developers add a counter log-based metric for 'payment declined' and alert when it spikes.",
    tip: "Count or chart something that appears in logs: log-based metric. Send logs somewhere else: sink (and grant its writer identity access). All projects in the organization: aggregated sink with include-children.",
    check: [["How long are logs kept in the _Default bucket by default?", "30 days, which you can change."], ["What must you do after creating a sink to BigQuery?", "Grant the sink's writer identity permission to write to the dataset."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Cloud Audit Logs: Admin Activity, Data Access, System Event and Policy Denied logs",
    body: [
      "Cloud Audit Logs answer the question 'who did what, where and when' for Google Cloud resources. They're written to Cloud Logging and each entry records the principal (user or service account), the method called (such as `v1.compute.instances.delete`), the resource, the time, the caller's IP address and whether the request succeeded. They're essential for security investigations, compliance and troubleshooting.",
      "There are four types. Admin Activity audit logs record API calls that modify configuration or metadata: creating or deleting a VM, changing an IAM policy, updating a bucket's settings. They're always enabled, can't be turned off, and are free. System Event audit logs record actions taken by Google systems rather than users, such as live migration of a VM or a managed instance group's autohealing; they are also always on and free.",
      "Data Access audit logs record API calls that read configuration or metadata (ADMIN_READ) and calls that read or write user-provided data (DATA_READ and DATA_WRITE), such as reading an object in Cloud Storage or querying a table. They can be very large, so they're disabled by default for most services, and you pay for the log volume beyond free allotments. BigQuery is the notable exception, where Data Access logs are enabled by default. You enable them per service, for a project, folder or organization, in the console under IAM and admin > Audit logs, or in the IAM policy's auditConfigs section. You can exempt specific principals.",
      "Policy Denied audit logs are written when Google Cloud denies access because of a security policy, such as a VPC Service Controls perimeter. They're generated by default, and you can exclude them if needed.",
      "To view audit logs you need the right role: Logs Viewer (roles/logging.viewer) can see Admin Activity and System Event logs, while Data Access logs are considered sensitive and require Private Logs Viewer (roles/logging.privateLogViewer). In Logs Explorer, filter on `logName` containing cloudaudit.googleapis.com, or use `protoPayload.methodName` and `protoPayload.authenticationInfo.principalEmail`."
    ],
    terms: [["Admin Activity audit logs", "Always-on logs of API calls that change resource configuration or metadata."], ["Data Access audit logs", "Logs of reads of configuration and reads or writes of user data, off by default for most services."], ["System Event audit logs", "Logs of actions taken by Google systems, such as live migration."], ["Private Logs Viewer", "The role needed to view Data Access audit logs."]],
    example: "After a production VM disappears, an engineer filters Admin Activity logs for methodName containing instances.delete and finds the principal and time within a minute. To meet a new regulation, the company then enables DATA_READ audit logs for Cloud Storage on its patient-records project only, keeping log costs under control.",
    tip: "Who changed or deleted something: Admin Activity (always on). Who read data: Data Access, which must be enabled first (except BigQuery). Viewing Data Access logs needs Private Logs Viewer.",
    check: [["Can you disable Admin Activity audit logs?", "No. They're always written and can't be turned off."], ["Why are Data Access logs disabled by default for most services?", "They can be very high volume and add cost, so you enable them where needed."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Troubleshooting with Error Reporting, Cloud Trace and Cloud Profiler",
    body: [
      "Beyond metrics and logs, Google Cloud Observability includes three tools aimed at application problems. Each answers a different question, and exam questions usually hinge on matching the question to the tool.",
      "Error Reporting answers 'what errors is my app throwing, and are they new?'. It analyzes logs from Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions, App Engine, GKE and Compute Engine (and errors reported through its API or client libraries), recognizes stack traces, and groups similar exceptions together with counts, first- and last-seen times and sample stack traces. It can notify you when a new error group appears, and you can mark errors as acknowledged or resolved. For many serverless services, it works automatically with no code changes, as long as exceptions are written to logs.",
      "Cloud Trace answers 'where is the time going in a request?'. It's a distributed tracing system: each request gets a trace made of spans for each step, such as the front end, a call to another service, and a database query, with their timing. It shows latency distributions and helps you find the slow service in a chain of microservices. Many Google services and load balancers produce trace data, and you instrument your own code with OpenTelemetry to send spans.",
      "Cloud Profiler answers 'which functions in my code use the most CPU or memory?'. It continuously samples running applications in production with low overhead and shows flame graphs of CPU time, heap usage and other resources by function. You add the profiling agent or library to your application. Use it to reduce cost and latency by optimizing hot code paths.",
      "A typical investigation combines them: an alerting policy fires on high latency, Cloud Trace shows the time is spent in one service, Cloud Profiler shows which function is using CPU there, Logs Explorer shows the related log entries, and Error Reporting shows whether a new exception started with the latest release. Other helpers include Active Assist recommendations, such as right-sizing idle VMs, and Network Intelligence Center's Connectivity Tests for network path problems."
    ],
    terms: [["Error Reporting", "A service that groups and counts application exceptions from logs and alerts on new ones."], ["Cloud Trace", "A distributed tracing service that shows request latency broken into spans."], ["Cloud Profiler", "A low-overhead continuous profiler that shows CPU and memory use by function."], ["Span", "One timed operation within a trace, such as a call to another service."]],
    example: "Checkout latency rises after a release. Cloud Trace shows most time in the pricing service, Cloud Profiler reveals a JSON-parsing function consuming most of its CPU, and Error Reporting shows a new timeout exception group that started with the same release. The team rolls back and fixes the parser.",
    tip: "Grouped exceptions: Error Reporting. Slow requests across services: Cloud Trace. Code-level CPU or memory hot spots: Cloud Profiler.",
    check: [["Which tool shows which microservice adds the most latency to a request?", "Cloud Trace, through the spans in each trace."], ["Does Error Reporting require code changes on Cloud Run?", "Usually not; it reads exceptions with stack traces from the service's logs."]]
  },
  {
    t: "IAM roles: basic, predefined and custom roles, and least privilege",
    body: [
      "In Google Cloud you never grant permissions directly; you grant roles, which are collections of permissions. A permission has the form service.resource.verb, such as `compute.instances.start` or `storage.objects.get`. Picking the right role is the core of least privilege: give each principal only the access its job requires, at the narrowest scope that works.",
      "Basic roles are the original, very broad roles. Viewer can read most resources. Editor can view, create, change and delete most resources. Owner has Editor's rights plus the ability to manage IAM policies and billing for the project. These roles include thousands of permissions across nearly every service, so Google recommends against them in production. Exam answers that grant Owner or Editor 'to make it work' are almost always wrong.",
      "Predefined roles are created and maintained by Google for specific services and jobs, and are updated as services add features. Examples include Compute Instance Admin (v1), Storage Object Viewer, BigQuery Data Editor, Cloud Run Developer, Kubernetes Engine Developer and Logs Viewer. They're the first choice in most situations. Role IDs look like `roles/storage.objectViewer`, and `gcloud iam roles describe roles/storage.objectViewer` lists the permissions a role contains.",
      "Custom roles contain exactly the permissions you choose, and you create them when no predefined role fits, for example a role that can start and stop VMs but not delete them. They can be defined at the organization or project level (not on folders), and you maintain them yourself: Google doesn't add new permissions to them. Create one with `gcloud iam roles create vmOperator --project=my-proj --permissions=compute.instances.start,compute.instances.stop,compute.instances.get,compute.instances.list` or from a YAML file. Some permissions can't be used in custom roles, and roles go through launch stages such as ALPHA, BETA and GA.",
      "Least privilege also means choosing the scope carefully. A role granted on one bucket is safer than the same role on the project. Pair roles with groups so that access follows team membership, review access regularly with the IAM recommender, and use IAM Conditions for temporary or restricted grants."
    ],
    terms: [["Basic roles", "The broad Owner, Editor and Viewer roles that apply across nearly all services."], ["Predefined role", "A Google-maintained role for a specific service and job, such as Storage Object Viewer."], ["Custom role", "A role you define with an exact list of permissions, at the organization or project level."], ["Least privilege", "Granting only the permissions needed, at the narrowest scope."]],
    example: "An operations contractor must restart VMs during maintenance windows. Instead of Editor, the admin creates a custom role with the start, stop, get and list permissions for instances, grants it to the contractor group on one project, and the contractor can't delete VMs or change anything else.",
    tip: "Prefer predefined roles; use custom roles only when no predefined role fits; avoid basic roles. Custom roles can't be created on folders and aren't updated automatically by Google.",
    check: [["Which basic role can change IAM policies on a project?", "Owner. Editor can't manage IAM policies."], ["At what levels can custom roles be created?", "The organization or a project, not folders."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Viewing and changing IAM allow policies with gcloud, and IAM Conditions",
    body: [
      "An allow policy is a JSON or YAML document attached to a resource. It contains a list of bindings, each with a role and a list of members, plus an etag and a version. Knowing its shape helps you read command output and exam scenarios.",
      "To view a policy: `gcloud projects get-iam-policy my-proj` (or `--format=json`). Similar get-iam-policy commands exist for folders, organizations, buckets (`gcloud storage buckets get-iam-policy gs://b`), service accounts, and many other resources. The simplest way to change one binding is `add-iam-policy-binding` or `remove-iam-policy-binding`, which read, modify and write the policy for you.",
      "For bulk changes you can download the policy, edit it, and upload it with `gcloud projects set-iam-policy my-proj policy.json`. Be careful: set-iam-policy replaces the entire policy with the file's contents, so if the file is stale or missing bindings you can remove access, even your own. The etag protects against concurrent edits: if someone changed the policy after you downloaded it, the upload is rejected and you must fetch it again.",
      "IAM Conditions let a binding apply only when an expression in Common Expression Language (CEL) is true. Common uses are time-limited access, such as `request.time < timestamp(\"2026-12-31T00:00:00Z\")`, and resource-based limits, such as allowing access only to resources whose name starts with a prefix or that carry a certain tag. Add one with `--condition='expression=...,title=...'` on add-iam-policy-binding. Conditions require policy version 3, and not every service or role supports every attribute, so check before relying on them. Basic roles can't be used with conditions.",
      "Remember what an allow policy can't do: it can't remove access granted higher in the hierarchy. For that, Google Cloud offers IAM deny policies, which explicitly block specific permissions for specific principals and are evaluated before allow policies. Principal access boundary policies are another way to limit which resources principals can access."
    ],
    terms: [["Binding", "One role and the list of members that hold it in an allow policy."], ["etag", "A version marker that prevents overwriting a policy someone else changed."], ["set-iam-policy", "A command that replaces a resource's entire allow policy with a file's contents."], ["IAM Condition", "A CEL expression that makes a role binding apply only in certain cases, such as before a date."]],
    example: "An auditor needs read access for two weeks. The admin runs add-iam-policy-binding with the Viewer role on the audit project and a condition that expires on the last day of the audit, so the access disappears automatically without anyone remembering to remove it.",
    tip: "For one change, use add- or remove-iam-policy-binding. set-iam-policy overwrites everything. Temporary access is an IAM Condition on request.time.",
    check: [["What happens if you run set-iam-policy with a file missing some bindings?", "Those bindings are removed, because the whole policy is replaced."], ["Which IAM feature grants a role only until a certain date?", "An IAM Condition using request.time in the binding."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Service accounts: creating them, granting roles, attaching them to resources, and default service accounts",
    body: [
      "A service account is an identity for a workload, not a person. Applications running on VMs, GKE, Cloud Run or Cloud Run functions use service accounts to call Google APIs. A service account has an email address like `app-sa@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. It is both a principal (it can be granted roles) and a resource (people can be granted roles on it, such as permission to use it).",
      "Create one with `gcloud iam service-accounts create app-sa --display-name=\"App\"`, then grant it roles on the resources it needs, for example `gcloud storage buckets add-iam-policy-binding gs://uploads --member=serviceAccount:app-sa@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com --role=roles/storage.objectCreator`. Create a separate service account for each application or component so each has only its own permissions and audit logs show which workload did what.",
      "Attaching a service account to a resource is how the workload gets credentials. For a VM, use `gcloud compute instances create ... --service-account=app-sa@... --scopes=cloud-platform`; code on the VM then gets short-lived access tokens from the metadata server automatically, with no keys to manage. Cloud Run services and functions take `--service-account` at deployment. Access scopes are a legacy VM mechanism that limits which APIs the VM's credentials can reach; Google's recommendation is to set the cloud-platform scope and control access with IAM roles.",
      "To attach a service account, you need the Service Account User role (roles/iam.serviceAccountUser) on that service account, because running code as it lets you use its permissions. This prevents someone with only permission to create VMs from borrowing a powerful service account. Service Account Admin lets you create and manage service accounts, which is a separate power.",
      "Some services create default service accounts, such as the Compute Engine default service account, `PROJECT_NUMBER-compute@developer.gserviceaccount.com`. Depending on your organization's settings, it may be granted the Editor role automatically, and VMs use it unless you pick another. Best practice is to create dedicated, least-privilege service accounts instead, and many organizations use an organization policy to stop the automatic Editor grant. Don't confuse these with service agents, which Google uses to run services on your behalf."
    ],
    terms: [["Service account", "An identity for applications and workloads, identified by an email address."], ["Service Account User", "A role that allows attaching or acting as a service account."], ["Metadata server", "An endpoint on each VM that provides short-lived tokens for the attached service account."], ["Compute Engine default service account", "An automatically created service account that VMs use unless another is specified."]],
    example: "A team deploys an invoice service to Cloud Run with its own service account, invoice-sa, which has only Secret Manager Secret Accessor on one secret and Cloud SQL Client on the project. When the logs show an unexpected read, they know exactly which service made it.",
    tip: "Workloads should use attached, dedicated service accounts, not keys and not the default Editor account. Permission errors when attaching a service account mean the user lacks Service Account User on it.",
    check: [["Why does attaching a service account to a VM require Service Account User?", "Because the VM's code can then use the service account's permissions, which is a form of acting as it."], ["How does code on a VM get credentials for its attached service account?", "From the metadata server, which issues short-lived access tokens automatically."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Service account impersonation, short-lived credentials and avoiding service account keys",
    body: [
      "A service account key is a long-lived private key file (JSON) that anyone can use to authenticate as the service account, from anywhere, until the key is deleted or disabled. Keys are one of the most common causes of cloud breaches because they get copied into source code, laptops, CI systems and chat messages. Google's guidance is to avoid creating keys whenever another option exists, and many organizations block key creation with the `iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation` organization policy.",
      "For workloads running on Google Cloud, the alternative is to attach a service account, as covered earlier, so the platform provides short-lived tokens. For people who need to act as a service account, for example to test its permissions or run an automation script, the alternative is impersonation.",
      "Impersonation means a principal uses its own credentials to obtain short-lived credentials for a service account. The principal needs the Service Account Token Creator role (roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator) on that service account. With gcloud you add `--impersonate-service-account=deploy-sa@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com` to a command, or set it for a whole configuration with `gcloud config set auth/impersonate_service_account deploy-sa@...`. Terraform and client libraries support impersonation too. The tokens typically expire after an hour, and audit logs record both the service account and the person who impersonated it, which makes the activity traceable.",
      "The IAM Service Account Credentials API is what issues these tokens: OAuth 2.0 access tokens, OpenID Connect ID tokens for calling services such as Cloud Run, and signed blobs or JWTs. Chains of impersonation (delegation) are possible but should be used sparingly.",
      "If a key must exist, for example for a third-party system that supports nothing else, manage it carefully: store it in a secret manager, rotate it regularly, set an expiry using the key expiry organization policy where available, monitor key usage, and delete keys that aren't used. If a key leaks, disable or delete it immediately and review audit logs for what it was used for. Removing the key from a repository doesn't make it invalid."
    ],
    terms: [["Service account key", "A long-lived private key that lets anyone holding it authenticate as the service account."], ["Impersonation", "Using your own identity to obtain short-lived credentials for a service account."], ["Service Account Token Creator", "The role that allows generating short-lived credentials for a service account."], ["Short-lived credentials", "Access or ID tokens that expire quickly, typically after about an hour."]],
    example: "A developer used to keep a key for deploy-sa on her laptop. Her team deletes the key, grants her group Service Account Token Creator on deploy-sa, and she now runs `gcloud config set auth/impersonate_service_account deploy-sa@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. Audit logs show each deployment with both her identity and the service account.",
    tip: "'Without creating a key' plus 'act as a service account' means Token Creator and impersonation. A leaked key must be disabled or deleted; deleting the commit isn't enough.",
    check: [["Which role is required to impersonate a service account?", "Service Account Token Creator on that service account."], ["What should you do first if a key is committed to a public repository?", "Disable or delete the key, then review audit logs for misuse."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Workload Identity Federation for GKE and for workloads outside Google Cloud",
    body: [
      "Workloads need identities too, and the goal is to give each one its own short-lived Google credentials without keys. Google Cloud offers two related features, both called Workload Identity Federation, one for GKE pods and one for workloads running elsewhere.",
      "Without special configuration, pods on a GKE node use the node's service account through the metadata server, so every pod on the node shares the same permissions. Workload Identity Federation for GKE fixes this. When it's enabled on the cluster (it's always on in Autopilot) and on the node pool, GKE runs a metadata server that gives each pod credentials for its Kubernetes service account. You then grant IAM roles directly to that Kubernetes service account using a principal identifier based on the project's workload identity pool (PROJECT_ID.svc.id.goog), or you link the Kubernetes service account to a Google service account by granting it the Workload Identity User role and annotating the Kubernetes service account. Either way, each app gets only the access its own identity is granted.",
      "Workload Identity Federation for external workloads lets applications outside Google Cloud authenticate without keys. Examples include CI/CD pipelines in GitHub Actions or GitLab, workloads on AWS or Azure, and on-premises systems with an OIDC or SAML identity provider. You create a workload identity pool and a provider that trusts the external identity provider, map attributes (such as the repository name) and add attribute conditions to restrict which external identities are accepted. The external workload exchanges its own token with Google's Security Token Service for a short-lived federated token, and then either uses it directly on resources where the federated principal has roles, or impersonates a service account.",
      "Both approaches remove long-lived keys from the picture, so there's nothing to leak or rotate, and access is tied to verifiable identities such as a specific repository or Kubernetes namespace. The exam typically presents a scenario with a CI pipeline or pods and asks for the most secure way to give access; the answer is the matching Workload Identity Federation feature, not a key stored as a secret."
    ],
    terms: [["Workload Identity Federation for GKE", "A GKE feature that gives each Kubernetes service account its own IAM identity and short-lived credentials."], ["Workload identity pool", "A container for external identities that Google Cloud trusts."], ["Workload identity pool provider", "The configuration that trusts a specific external identity provider, such as GitHub's OIDC issuer."], ["Security Token Service", "The Google service that exchanges external tokens for short-lived federated tokens."]],
    example: "A company's GitHub Actions workflow deploys to Cloud Run. A workload identity pool provider trusts GitHub's OIDC tokens only for the repository example-org/shop, and that identity can impersonate deploy-sa. No key exists anywhere, and a fork of the repository can't obtain credentials.",
    tip: "Pods needing their own Google identity: Workload Identity Federation for GKE. CI/CD or other clouds without keys: Workload Identity Federation with a pool and provider. Keys in secrets are the wrong answer.",
    check: [["Why is using the node's service account for all pods a problem?", "Every pod on the node shares the same permissions, breaking least privilege."], ["What restricts which GitHub repositories can use a workload identity pool provider?", "Attribute mappings and attribute conditions on the provider, such as a condition on the repository claim."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login for secure administrative access",
    body: [
      "Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) implements a zero-trust access model: instead of trusting anyone on the right network, it checks who the user is (and optionally their device and context) on every request, and only then lets the request through to the application or VM. It removes the need for VPNs and bastion hosts for many access patterns.",
      "For web applications, IAP sits in front of an external Application Load Balancer's backend service, App Engine, or Cloud Run. Users sign in with their Google identity, and IAP allows the request only if they have the IAP-secured Web App User role on the app. The app still has to accept traffic only from the load balancer, typically with firewall rules, so users can't bypass IAP. With Access Context Manager you can add context-aware conditions, such as requiring a company-managed device or a certain IP range.",
      "For administrative access to VMs, IAP TCP forwarding tunnels SSH, RDP or other TCP connections to VMs that have no external IP address. Users need the IAP-secured Tunnel User role, and firewall rules must allow the IAP range 35.235.240.0/20 to the port. Commands like `gcloud compute ssh vm-1 --tunnel-through-iap` or `gcloud compute start-iap-tunnel vm-1 3389 --local-host-port=localhost:3389` set up the tunnel.",
      "OS Login complements IAP by controlling who can log in to Linux VMs once connected. It links Linux user accounts to Google identities and grants access through IAM: Compute OS Login for normal users, Compute OS Admin Login for users who need sudo. OS Login can enforce two-step verification, and because it uses IAM, access is removed as soon as a role is revoked or an account is disabled. That's much easier to govern than SSH keys scattered in project metadata. For users outside the organization, the Compute OS Login External User role is also needed at the organization level.",
      "Together they give a clean model: no public IPs on VMs, IAP decides who can reach the VM, OS Login decides who can log in and with what privileges, and audit logs record it all."
    ],
    terms: [["Identity-Aware Proxy", "A service that authorizes each request based on user identity and context before it reaches an app or VM."], ["IAP-secured Web App User", "The role that allows a user through IAP to a protected web app."], ["IAP-secured Tunnel User", "The role that allows TCP forwarding through IAP to VMs."], ["Compute OS Admin Login", "The OS Login role that grants login with sudo privileges."]],
    example: "A company retires its VPN for an internal HR app by putting it behind an external Application Load Balancer with IAP enabled, granting IAP-secured Web App User to the hr-staff group, and requiring company-managed devices through an access level. Admins reach the app's VMs through IAP tunnels with OS Login.",
    tip: "Identity-based access to an internal web app without VPN: IAP. SSH without external IPs: IAP TCP forwarding. SSH access controlled by IAM: OS Login.",
    check: [["Which role lets a user run sudo on a VM using OS Login?", "Compute OS Admin Login."], ["What stops users from bypassing IAP to reach the backend directly?", "Firewall rules that allow traffic only from the load balancer and IAP ranges, and no public IPs on the backends."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Encryption: Google default encryption, Cloud KMS customer-managed keys and customer-supplied keys",
    body: [
      "All data stored in Google Cloud is encrypted at rest by default, with no action on your part. Google uses envelope encryption: data is encrypted with data encryption keys, which are themselves encrypted with key encryption keys held in Google's internal key management systems. Data in transit between Google data centers and to Google services is also encrypted. For many workloads, this default encryption with Google-owned and Google-managed keys is enough.",
      "Some organizations must control the keys themselves, for compliance or to be able to cut off access to data. Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) let you create key rings and keys in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) and tell services such as Cloud Storage, Compute Engine disks, BigQuery, Cloud SQL and GKE to use them. Google still performs the encryption, but you control the key's lifecycle: rotation schedule, enabling and disabling versions, destroying versions, and IAM on the key. If you disable the key, the service can't decrypt the data. Each service uses a service agent that needs the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role on the key.",
      "Cloud KMS keys can be protected in software, in hardware security modules (Cloud HSM, validated to FIPS 140-2 Level 3), or kept outside Google in a supported external key manager (Cloud External Key Manager). Key usage is recorded in Cloud Audit Logs. Destroying a key version is scheduled after a waiting period, to protect against mistakes; once destroyed, data encrypted with it can't be recovered.",
      "Customer-supplied encryption keys (CSEK) are supported by a few services, notably Cloud Storage and Compute Engine disks. You generate the key yourself and supply it with each request; Google uses it in memory and doesn't store it. If you lose the key, Google can't recover your data. CSEK gives maximum control but adds operational burden, which is why CMEK is the usual answer.",
      "Finally, client-side encryption means you encrypt data before sending it to Google Cloud, so Google only ever sees ciphertext. This is your responsibility entirely and is independent of the server-side options."
    ],
    terms: [["Default encryption", "Automatic encryption at rest of all data with Google-managed keys."], ["CMEK", "Customer-managed encryption keys in Cloud KMS that you control and Google services use."], ["CSEK", "Customer-supplied encryption keys that you provide with each request and Google doesn't store."], ["Key ring", "A grouping of Cloud KMS keys in one location."]],
    example: "A healthcare company must be able to make patient data unreadable on demand. It creates a key in Cloud KMS, sets it as the default CMEK on its records bucket and grants the Cloud Storage service agent Encrypter/Decrypter on the key. Security can disable the key to block all decryption instantly.",
    tip: "'Control rotation and be able to disable the key, but Google does the encryption' is CMEK. 'Google must never store the key' is CSEK. Nothing required means default encryption.",
    check: [["What permission does a service need to use a CMEK key?", "Its service agent needs the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role on the key."], ["What happens if you lose a customer-supplied key?", "The data encrypted with it can't be recovered, because Google doesn't store the key."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Secret Manager for passwords, API keys and certificates",
    body: [
      "Applications need secrets: database passwords, third-party API keys, TLS private keys and tokens. Putting them in source code, container images, plain environment variables in deployment files or configuration buckets is risky, because anyone who can read those places can read the secret, and it's hard to rotate them. Secret Manager is Google Cloud's managed service for storing secrets securely.",
      "A secret in Secret Manager is a named container, and the actual values are stored as versions. When you rotate a password you add a new version and, once everything uses it, disable or destroy the old one. Applications can request a specific version number or `latest`. Create a secret with `gcloud secrets create db-password --replication-policy=automatic` and add a value with `gcloud secrets versions add db-password --data-file=pw.txt`. Replication can be automatic (Google chooses locations) or user-managed (you choose regions, for data residency), and secrets are encrypted by default, with optional CMEK.",
      "Access is controlled by IAM. The key role is Secret Manager Secret Accessor (roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor), which allows reading secret values; grant it to the specific service account of the workload, on the specific secret, not the whole project. Secret Manager Admin manages secrets but you should grant it only to administrators. Every access is recorded in Cloud Audit Logs (Data Access logs, when enabled), so you can see which identity read a secret.",
      "Integrations make secrets easy to use. Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions can mount a secret as a file or expose it as an environment variable at deployment, for example `gcloud run deploy api --set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:latest`. GKE can read secrets through the Secret Manager add-on or the Secrets Store CSI driver, and any application can call the Secret Manager API with client libraries. Rotation schedules can send Pub/Sub notifications to trigger your own rotation code, and secrets can have expiration times.",
      "Secret Manager is for application secrets. It's different from Cloud KMS, which manages encryption keys used to encrypt data but never gives you the raw key material of a software or HSM key."
    ],
    terms: [["Secret", "A named container in Secret Manager that holds versions of a sensitive value."], ["Secret version", "An immutable value of a secret; new versions are added to rotate."], ["Secret Manager Secret Accessor", "The IAM role that allows reading a secret's value."], ["Replication policy", "Whether a secret's data is replicated automatically or only to regions you choose."]],
    example: "A payments team stores its processor API key in Secret Manager, grants Secret Accessor on that one secret to the checkout service's service account, and deploys to Cloud Run with --set-secrets. When the processor rotates the key, the team adds a new version, and the next deployment picks up latest.",
    tip: "Passwords and API keys belong in Secret Manager with Secret Accessor granted to the workload's service account. Cloud KMS is for encryption keys, not for storing app passwords.",
    check: [["How do you rotate a secret in Secret Manager?", "Add a new version, move applications to it, then disable or destroy the old version."], ["Which role should a Cloud Run service's service account have to read a secret?", "Secret Manager Secret Accessor on that specific secret."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Cloud Storage access: uniform bucket-level access, public access prevention and signed URLs",
    body: [
      "Cloud Storage has two access control systems. IAM grants roles on the project or on a bucket (and, with managed folders, on prefixes), such as Storage Object Viewer, Storage Object Creator or Storage Admin. Access control lists (ACLs) are an older, finer-grained system that can grant access on individual objects. Using both makes it hard to know who can read what.",
      "Uniform bucket-level access disables ACLs on a bucket so that only IAM (at the bucket and project levels, plus managed folders) controls access. It's recommended for almost all buckets and is required for some features, such as IAM Conditions on buckets and managed folders. Once enabled, you have a limited window to turn it off before it becomes permanent. Fine-grained access is the mode that keeps ACLs, for special cases like per-object sharing in legacy apps.",
      "A bucket or object becomes public when access is granted to allUsers (anyone on the internet) or allAuthenticatedUsers (anyone with a Google account). Public access prevention blocks these grants. You can enforce it on a bucket, or across projects, folders or the whole organization with the `storage.publicAccessPrevention` organization policy constraint. With it enforced, even an administrator can't make data public by mistake; hosting a public website then needs a bucket in a project where it isn't enforced, or a load balancer and Cloud CDN setup designed for that.",
      "Signed URLs give time-limited access to a specific object to someone who doesn't have a Google identity, without making the bucket public. The URL contains a signature created with a service account's credentials and an expiry time, up to 7 days for V4 signatures. Anyone holding the URL can perform the specified action (for example GET to download or PUT to upload) until it expires. Create one with `gcloud storage sign-url gs://bucket/report.pdf --duration=1h --private-key-file=key.json` or, better, with impersonation instead of a key file. Signed policy documents do the same for browser form uploads.",
      "Other controls include retention policies and holds, Requester Pays, VPC Service Controls perimeters around storage, and CMEK for encryption. Data Access audit logs record who read objects when enabled."
    ],
    terms: [["Uniform bucket-level access", "A bucket setting that disables ACLs so only IAM controls access."], ["Public access prevention", "A setting or organization policy that blocks access grants to allUsers and allAuthenticatedUsers."], ["Signed URL", "A URL that grants time-limited access to an object without requiring a Google identity."], ["allUsers", "A special principal meaning anyone on the internet."]],
    example: "A law firm needs to share a 2 GB file with a client who has no Google account. The bucket has uniform bucket-level access and public access prevention enforced, so the admin generates a V4 signed URL valid for 24 hours. The client downloads the file, and the link stops working the next day.",
    tip: "Only IAM, no ACLs: uniform bucket-level access. Never public, even by mistake: enforce public access prevention (org policy for everything). Temporary access for someone without an account: signed URL.",
    check: [["What is the maximum lifetime of a V4 signed URL?", "7 days."], ["Does uniform bucket-level access by itself stop a bucket from being made public?", "No. IAM can still grant allUsers access unless public access prevention is enforced."]]
  },
  {
    t: "Policy Troubleshooter, IAM recommendations and audit roles for reviewing access",
    body: [
      "Granting access is only half the job; you also need to understand and review it. Google Cloud's Policy Intelligence tools help you answer 'why can or can't this person do this?', 'who has access to this?', and 'what access is no longer needed?'.",
      "Policy Troubleshooter answers the first question. You give it a principal, a resource and a permission (for example `bigquery.tables.getData` on a dataset), and it evaluates all the relevant allow policies up the hierarchy, deny policies and principal access boundary policies. It reports whether access is granted, which binding grants it, or which binding is missing or which deny rule blocks it. It's the first stop when a user says 'I get permission denied'. You can also troubleshoot from the error message link in many console pages.",
      "Policy Analyzer answers 'who has access to what'. It searches IAM policies across a project, folder or organization, for example to list every principal that can read a sensitive bucket, including through group membership and inherited grants. It relies on Cloud Asset Inventory, which also lets you search and export resource metadata and IAM policies.",
      "The IAM recommender, part of Active Assist, answers 'what can be removed'. It compares the permissions each principal has with the permissions they have actually used over a recent observation period (90 days), and recommends removing unused roles or replacing broad roles with smaller ones. Applying recommendations steadily moves you toward least privilege. Related insights flag things like service accounts that haven't been used for a long time, which may be good candidates to disable.",
      "For people who review access rather than change it, use read-only roles. Security Reviewer (roles/iam.securityReviewer) can list resources and view IAM policies without changing them, which suits auditors. Viewer-type roles such as Logs Viewer, Private Logs Viewer (for Data Access audit logs) and Cloud Asset Viewer support investigations. Security Command Center adds a centralized view of misconfigurations and threats, such as public buckets or overly permissive firewall rules, across the organization."
    ],
    terms: [["Policy Troubleshooter", "A tool that explains whether a principal has a permission on a resource and why."], ["Policy Analyzer", "A tool that finds which principals have which access across the hierarchy."], ["IAM recommender", "An Active Assist feature that suggests removing or reducing roles based on permission usage."], ["Security Reviewer", "A read-only role for listing resources and viewing IAM policies."]],
    example: "Each quarter, a security engineer applies IAM recommender suggestions that shrink Editor grants to predefined roles, runs Policy Analyzer to list everyone who can read the payroll bucket, and grants the external auditors Security Reviewer at the organization. When a developer reports a permission error, Policy Troubleshooter shows the missing BigQuery role in seconds.",
    tip: "'Why is this user denied' is Policy Troubleshooter. 'Who can access this resource' is Policy Analyzer. 'Which roles are unused' is the IAM recommender. 'Auditor can view but not change' is Security Reviewer.",
    check: [["What inputs does Policy Troubleshooter need?", "A principal, a resource and a permission."], ["What data does the IAM recommender use?", "Permission usage over a recent observation period compared with the permissions granted."]]
  }
